WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:04.830
Kids love Scooby-Doo, but there's a lot of
questionable aspects to the mystery series

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:07.320
that they might not catch until after they've
grown up.

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:11.620
Here's the most suspect stuff in Scooby-Doo
shows that only grown-ups pick up on.

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:16.900
Scooby-Doo is so familiar that we all tend
to overlook its central conceit: one of the

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.000
main characters is a talking dog.

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:23.699
But even more unbelievable than a Great Dane
who speaks and experiences human emotions

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:25.710
are the reactions of the people around him.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.380
"I was saving this for an emergency."

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:30.200
"This is an emergency.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.260
That's the last hamburger."

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:38.239
Shaggy, Fred, Velma, Daphne, and the various
criminals they bust are never impressed or

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:39.580
freaked out by Scooby.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:44.280
Scooby's human-like intelligence and ability
to speak should probably make him a marvel

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:45.880
of nature, science, or both.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:49.840
But everybody converses with Scooby like it's
the most normal thing in the world.

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:56.020
Talking dog aside, the cast of most Scooby-Doo
shows is led by the same four humans of Mystery,

00:00:56.020 --> 00:01:00.800
Incorporated: Fred Jones, Daphne Blake, Velma
Dinkley, and Norville "Shaggy" Rogers.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:05.759
Together, this unlikely foursome travels the
land in their psychedelic van, solving mysteries

00:01:05.759 --> 00:01:07.770
and debunking supernatural phenomena.

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:12.130
That much is obvious, but what isn't clear
is just how old they're all supposed to be.

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:16.210
They'd have to be at least 16 to drive that
sweet van, and making them teens would put

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:18.920
them closer to the shows' target demographic
of children.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:21.540
Plus, the villains love to call them "meddling
kids."

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:26.000
But it also seems like they must be at least
in their 20s, since teenagers wouldn't likely

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.500
be found living unsupervised on the open road.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:31.940
And speaking of the open road, why are they
out on their own in the first place?

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:35.000
Are they homeless, or possibly fleeing something
ghastly?

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:39.560
Since the show was originally produced in
the late '60s and early '70s, one possibility

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:44.439
could've even been that Fred or Shaggy was
dodging the Vietnam draft, and Daphne and

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:45.900
Velma just went along for the ride.

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:46.980
"Zoinks!

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:47.780
We’re gonna sink!”

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:48.980
“I’ll fix it!”

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:51.900
“Good work Scoob."

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:58.299
Kids may not pick up on the canned laughter
in each episode of Scooby-Doo, but adults

00:01:58.299 --> 00:02:02.490
can't help but wonder what business a laugh
track has on an animated children's show.

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:07.240
True, the laughter may help remind young viewers
that despite its spooky situations, Scooby-Doo

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:09.060
is supposed to be a light-hearted comedy.

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:13.300
But a laugh track suggests that the show was
filmed in front of a live studio audience,

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:15.660
which isn't possible with a cartoon like Scooby-Doo.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:19.620
Then again, in keeping with the spirit of
the show, maybe those laughs are coming from

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:23.820
ghosts, or are part of an evil plot hatched
by a corrupt amusement park owner.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:28.180
While kids may not pick up on some of the
show's less-than-subtle stoner vibes, among

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:32.840
certain adult viewers, Scooby-Doo cartoons
have long been beloved due to Shaggy and Scooby's

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:33.840
telltale habits.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:38.930
A children's cartoon may not ever explicitly
state that its characters are marajuana enthusiasts,

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:42.980
but the clues in Scooby-Doo are bountiful
enough that the Mystery Machine doesn't exactly

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:45.740
need a "Legalize It!" bumper sticker to make
the theme clear.

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:50.520
For example, Shaggy and Scooby can sometimes
be seen emerging from a big smoke cloud.

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:54.299
Additionally, the pair is obsessed with food,
particularly junk food.

00:02:54.299 --> 00:02:58.700
It's almost as though these two giggling slackers,
one of whom wears bell-bottom pants and sports

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:01.000
unkempt hair, always have the munchies.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:05.439
Which begs the question: if Shaggy smokes
marijuana with his dog, isn't that animal

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:05.940
abuse?

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:10.500
Goal number one for Shaggy and Scooby has
always been to eat as much as possible, and

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:14.569
the two frequently care more about finding
their next meal than doing their job of solving

00:03:14.569 --> 00:03:15.569
mysteries.

00:03:15.569 --> 00:03:20.250
But while the duo is constantly eating, adults
may notice that they actually have a few dietary

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:20.740
restrictions.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:25.300
In some of Scooby-Doo's more recent episodes,
Shaggy often expresses his desire for veggie

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:28.940
sandwiches and other substitutes for typical
fast food staples.

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:34.700
This is no accident Shaggy moved away from
meat in 2002 at the request of the guy who

00:03:34.709 --> 00:03:35.730
provided his voice.

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:40.769
Legendary disc jockey Casey Kasem was a noted
vegan, and he wanted to use his platform as

00:03:40.769 --> 00:03:44.800
Shaggy to educate kids about the benefits
of a vegetarian diet.

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:48.220
"There's aren't burgers."

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:50.620
"We changed managers last week.

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:51.560
Now we’re organic vegan."

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:57.080
Every episode of Scooby-Doo seems to follow
a formula: the gang drives through the night,

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:01.360
comes across a potentially haunted location,
and gets down to business looking for clues.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:05.680
Then, although it's never a good idea to go
off alone in a dangerous place, the four humans

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:07.500
inevitably decide to split up.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:11.599
And if things weren't already scary enough,
Fred, their self-appointed leader, always

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:13.520
has to go and make things even creepier.

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:17.180
Whenever the pairings are made, Fred's the
one who decides who will go off with whom.

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:22.060
Invariably, he pairs himself with Daphne,
the subject of his intense romantic interest.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:26.040
No matter what case they're investigating,
Fred never fails to get Daphne alone with

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:28.980
him in darkened rooms, under the guise of
doing their jobs.

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:29.840
What a creep!

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:34.700
Adding a young, energetic character to revitalize
a show that's getting a little long in the

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:39.340
tooth is a well-known television tradition,
and one that almost always fails miserably.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:44.020
On Scooby-Doo, this sort of cynical attempt
to boost ratings resulted in one of the most

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:47.560
universally loathed characters in cartoon
history, Scrappy-Doo.

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:52.210
A pint-sized version of Scooby, Scrappy was
clearly supposed to be cute, but wound up

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:53.180
just being annoying.

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:57.440
Although he's meant to serve as a contrast
to Scooby's laid-back, fraidy-cat energy,

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:01.800
Scrappy-Doo comes across as aggressive and
perpetually angling for a fist-fight.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:06.200
Not only is he a character nobody wanted or
asked for, but adults may be concerned about

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:10.640
Scrappy's excessively violent tendencies,
and wonder whether he might even be a bit

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:11.140
disturbed.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:17.120
Have you ever noticed that Scooby-Doo seems
to take place in a bizarre world of meteorological

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:19.860
anomalies, where it's always dark and seemingly
cold?

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:23.800
While kids may not notice that we rarely see
the sun shine on the Mystery, Incorporated

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:28.420
crew, adults may question the persistent nighttime
of the extended Scooby-Doo universe.

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:33.300
Additionally, the near-omnipresent fog suggests
a perpetually cool climate, as does Fred's

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:37.930
heavy-looking shirt, and Velma's choice to
always wear a cozy orange turtleneck sweater.

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:42.410
But Shaggy's always in a T-shirt, which begs
the question: just what temperature is it

00:05:42.410 --> 00:05:42.900
in this world?

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:48.360
When it began, Scooby-Doo was a silly, formulaic
cartoon about a dog and some young people

00:05:48.370 --> 00:05:52.560
encountering ghosts, which always turned out
to be shady business owners trying to scare

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:53.680
people off their property.

00:05:53.980 --> 00:05:58.700
But starting in the '70s, to mix things up,
writers and animators had the mystery-solvers

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:03.730
interact with various guest stars, whose real-world
counterparts could be living, dead, or even

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:04.220
fictional.

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:08.620
For example, in one installment, Scooby and
his friends meet Laurel and Hardy, the famous

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:09.400
comedy duo.

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:13.240
Those are actual people who lived, but both
died long before the episode aired.

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:17.890
On other occasions, the Scooby-Doo crew met
Sonny and Cher, Don Knotts, Josie and the

00:06:17.890 --> 00:06:19.320
Pussycats, and Batman.

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:23.440
The presence of some of those guest stars
would suggest that the Scooby characters are

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:26.040
real, since they interacted with actual living
humans.

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:29.780
But if some of those humans were dead, does
that mean the Mystery Machine is unstuck from

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:30.280
time?

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:31.360
And is Batman real too?

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:35.900
Fiction and real-life are confusingly blurred,
making the "reality" of Scooby-Doo a little

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:36.900
tough to define.

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:41.680
For young Scooby-Doo viewers, it may not be
obvious that the episodes tend to follow similar

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:44.800
trajectories, which always lead to a version
of the same ending.

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:48.740
What was thought to be a haunted building
turns out to not be so haunted after all.

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:53.180
The ghosts, goblins, spooks, and specters
that scared the Mystery, Incorporated crew

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:58.480
so badly are revealed to be some combination
of projections, shadows, sound effects, and

00:06:58.480 --> 00:06:59.260
masked bad guys.

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:04.060
In other words, Scooby-Doo constantly reiterates
that all of that spooky stuff isn't real,

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:07.680
and that there is always a logical explanation
for any supernatural occurrence.

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:12.060
Most of the time, the mystery the characters
solve is revealed to be the plot of a developer

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:14.480
or land owner hoping to scare people off their
land.

00:07:14.650 --> 00:07:18.540
Because greed is usually the only real scary
thing in the Scooby-Doo universe.

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:23.120
Yet every time the Mystery, Incorporated team
heads out on a new job, they seem to completely

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:27.380
forget this important fact, and the dozens
of normal jerks they've unmasked in the past.

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:32.300
Every episode features the gang overreacting
to what they think is a ghost or monster,

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:33.560
leading to Shaggy screaming,

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:34.840
"Let's get out of here!"

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:39.780
Then Shaggy and Scooby, and sometimes the
entire gang, inevitably flee in terror.

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:44.160
Either they have terrible memories, or their
fear of ghosts is so severe that they ignore

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:44.820
all logic.

00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:48.200
Which kind of makes you wonder why they're
in the supernatural detective business to

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:48.780
begin with.

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:52.380
Based on what happens during every single
mystery they've encountered, one of them ought

00:07:52.390 --> 00:07:56.620
to walk right up to the monster or ghost the
first time they see them and tear off that

00:07:56.620 --> 00:07:59.120
mask, but that thought never seems to occur
to them.

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:03.020
But of course, if the characters actually
learned from their previous cases, there'd

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:04.020
be no more show.

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:08.820
Apart from mowing lawns and selling lemonade,
most kids don't worry too much about putting

00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:10.020
cash in their pocket.

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:14.070
Grown-ups, though, are concerned with earning
a living to provide necessities like food,

00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:15.120
shelter, and clothing.

00:08:15.470 --> 00:08:19.800
So naturally, adults watching Scooby-Doo might
have some questions about how business is

00:08:19.810 --> 00:08:21.900
conducted at Mystery, Incorporated.

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:27.340
At first glance, Mystery, Incorporated seems
to be a legitimate operation, with the "Incorporated"

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:31.830
part of the name indicating that the detective
firm is registered with an oversight agency

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:32.900
and pays taxes.

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:37.439
But despite the official-sounding name, adult
viewers may wonder exactly how this company

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:38.540
is able to operate.

00:08:38.680 --> 00:08:42.960
Although money rarely changes hands, business
still appears to be booming, at least enough

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:46.740
for the firm to afford a company car or in
this case, a groovy van.

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:51.600
One wonders what sort of wage the four humans
earn, and whether or not Mystery, Incorporated

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:54.420
offers health, vision, and dental benefits.

00:08:54.880 --> 00:09:00.180
In classic episodes of Scooby-Doo, the dedicated
detectives of Mystery, Incorporated navigate

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:04.690
through the many backroads and country highways
of America, forever in search of their next

00:09:04.690 --> 00:09:05.500
sleuthing gig.

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:09.580
But if you look closely, that straightforward
premise may have some dark undertones.

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:13.939
In the world of Scooby-Doo, all the buildings
seem to be falling apart, and the land is

00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:17.300
dotted with closed amusement parks and long-abandoned
ghost towns.

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:21.699
All of this, coupled with Shaggy's sheer relief
whenever he comes across food, suggests that

00:09:21.699 --> 00:09:25.829
these young entrepreneurs are trying to get
by in a world that has taken a pretty significant

00:09:25.829 --> 00:09:26.870
turn for the worse.

00:09:26.870 --> 00:09:31.490
Perhaps a severe economic depression left
the country in shambles, or some kind of natural

00:09:31.490 --> 00:09:35.610
or man-made catastrophe prompted people to
flee all those small towns.

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:39.470
The show may be for kids, but all the signs
seem to indicate that Scooby-Doo might be

00:09:39.470 --> 00:09:42.080
a glimpse at life in a post-apocalyptic nightmare.

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:47.380
Anyone who's caught a few episodes of Scooby-Doo
over the past 50 years probably recalls the

00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:49.540
words to its bouncy, memorable theme song:

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:53.930
The theme song seems to imply that Scooby-Doo
spends most of the show being hard to locate,

00:09:53.930 --> 00:09:57.980
and that the Mystery, Incorporated team is
constantly searching for him so that they

00:09:57.980 --> 00:09:58.900
can get to work.

00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:01.300
But that's not actually what the show is about
at all.

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:05.960
Scooby-Doo never disappears during the episodes,
and he and Shaggy are never apart for long.

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:10.560
Sure, Scooby may run away from scary stuff,
but Shaggy is always with him, making the

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:14.440
song's suggestion that Scooby is constantly
missing a real mystery.

